INT. ORIENT NIGHT TRAIN - DINING CAR - NIGHT

1938. White tablecloths. Silver coffee pots. Lamplight swaying with the rails. Beyond the windows, black hills and distant farm lights.

CLARA WINTERBOURNE, 28, emerald gown, gloves, sips tea at a corner table. Across from her, ANTON VASS, 32, dinner jacket, a pianist's long fingers and a sad smile. PEPE, 50s, waiter, white jacket, hovers with a pot.

ANTON
You're quiet tonight.

CLARA
The train does that to me.

ANTON
It's the rhythm. It makes you think of everything you've left.

CLARA
Or everything you're going to.

ANTON
You sound like a woman with a plan.

CLARA
I sound like a woman with a ticket.

ANTON
To where?

CLARA
Vienna.

ANTON
Ah. Concert season.

CLARA
Not for the concerts.

ANTON
Then what?

CLARA
A wedding.

Anton laughs lightly. It stops.

ANTON
Whose?

CLARA
Mine.

ANTON
Congratulations. To whom?

CLARA
A man named Julian Vass.

Anton goes very still. Pepe backs away from the table with the coffee pot, silent as a cat.

ANTON
Julian Vass is my brother.

CLARA
I know.

ANTON
You've never mentioned him.

CLARA
You've never asked what I was running toward.

ANTON
And tonight, on this train, on this particular train, you decided to sit at my table.

CLARA
I decided to see what kind of man hasn't spoken to his brother in six years.

ANTON
And what kind is that?

CLARA
The kind who plays at midnight so no one hears him.

Anton lifts his teacup. Sets it down without drinking.

ANTON
Do you love him?

CLARA
I haven't met him.

ANTON
Then why?

CLARA
He writes beautifully. And he talks about you.

ANTON
What does he say?

CLARA
That you were the better pianist.

Anton closes his eyes. The train sways. Pepe, from across the room, drops a spoon.

ANTON
Clara.

CLARA
Yes.

ANTON
Would you like to hear what I play at midnight?

CLARA
(softly)
Very much.
